WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.300
Automatic storage duration is one of the most useful safety features in C++.

2
00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:11.640
A local object begins its lifetime when execution reaches its construction and ends when its scope exits.

3
00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:19.740
Destructors run automatically, so local containers, locks, smart pointers, and resource wrappers can clean up without a separate manual release path.

4
00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:24.560
This makes simple stack lifetimes easy to review and efficient in practice.

5
00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:27.240
The safety boundary is the scope.

6
00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:36.540
A pointer, reference, iterator, span, string view, or other view into a local object must not outlive that local object.

7
00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:43.600
Returning a reference to a local variable is unsafe because the referenced object is gone once the function returns.

8
00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:48.420
Storing the address of a local buffer in a longer-lived object has the same problem.

9
00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:55.560
The code may compile and may even appear to work during a test, but the access no longer refers to a valid object.

10
00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:58.960
Temporaries also require lifetime awareness.

11
00:00:59.060 --> 00:01:06.660
Binding, forwarding, or storing references to values that exist only for a short expression can create subtle dangling access.

12
00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:13.080
Return values are normally safe when returned by value, because the returned object is constructed for the caller.

13
00:01:13.180 --> 00:01:20.060
The danger appears when code tries to return or store access into an object whose lifetime remains local to the callee.

14
00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:26.340
Stack objects are often the simplest and safest choice when ownership is local and bounded.

15
00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:29.480
They become risky when access escapes the scope.

16
00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:35.740
During review, ask whether every borrowed stack reference remains shorter-lived than the object it observes.

17
00:01:36.020 --> 00:01:39.140
As code evolves, revisit those assumptions.

18
00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:47.960
A helper that once used a local buffer only during the call may later cache a view, schedule a callback, or return a reference for convenience.

19
00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:53.820
That is the moment a simple stack lifetime becomes a cross-boundary contract.

20
00:01:53.920 --> 00:02:00.080
Keeping stack borrows narrow, local, and obvious helps prevent those quiet lifetime extensions.
